#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
micron-terminald — browser terminal service for Micron OS.

Real Linux shells on server-side pseudo-terminals, streamed to the Web UI's
xterm.js over a WebSocket. Python standard library only (it ships on the ISO
and must work offline).

Trust model
-----------
* The service never decides *who* may have a shell. The management plane
  (PHP, which owns users, roles and sessions) asks for a one-time ticket over
  the control socket /run/micron/terminal.sock (mode 0660 root:www-data,
  SO_PEERCRED-checked). A ticket names the Web UI user, the privilege level
  (standard | admin), the browser Origin, the client address and — for a
  reattach — the session it may attach to. It is valid once, for a few
  seconds.
* The WebSocket listener binds 127.0.0.1 only; Apache proxies /terminal/ws to
  it. A connection must present an unused ticket, the exact Origin the ticket
  was issued for, and come from the same client address (X-Forwarded-For as
  set by the local proxy). Anything else is refused and audited.
* Standard sessions run as the unprivileged account `micron-term` with
  per-process resource limits. Admin sessions run as root and have a hard
  lifetime. Neither is ever started from a browser-supplied command: the
  shell is the account's login shell, nothing else.
* Keystrokes and output are never logged. The audit log records who, when,
  which level, how it ended, and every refusal.

Protocol (WebSocket, RFC 6455)
------------------------------
browser → server  text  {"t":"i","d":"<input>"} | {"t":"r","c":cols,"r":rows} | {"t":"p"}
server → browser  binary  terminal output (raw bytes)
                  text    {"t":"hello",...} | {"t":"exit","code":n} | {"t":"closed","reason":...}
                          | {"t":"notice","msg":...} | {"t":"pong"}
"""
